<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bielefeld</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bielefeld is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, on a pass through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. It was founded around 1214 by Count Hermann IV of Ravensberg below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparrenburg">Sparrenburg</a> castle and became the main town of the county. Linen weaving and trade made it prosperous from the 16th century, and it later grew into an industrial centre for textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machines</a> and food.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bielefeld.jpg" alt="Bielefeld" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zefram · CC BY 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bielefeld_Theater.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bielefeld was founded in 1214 by Count Hermann IV of Ravensberg to guard a pass across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. As a minor member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> it became the city of linen, known for its bleachfields into the 19th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648 made Ravensberg, from 1719 Minden-Ravensberg, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> lost its western territories to French control in 1807, Bielefeld belonged to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>; it returned to Prussia in 1813, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Province of Westphalia</a>.</p><p>The Cologne-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> railway opened in 1849, and the Bozi brothers built the first large mechanised spinning mill in 1851. Metal works began to open in the 1860s. Dürkoppwerke, founded in 1867 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machine</a> repair company, employed 1,665 people in 1892, and the Dürkopp car was produced from 1898 to 1927. In 1923, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, the town's savings bank printed emergency money on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, linen and velvet, now collectable as Stoffgeld.</p><p>The city's synagogue was burned in 1938 during the Kristallnacht pogrom. In 1944 and 1945 American and British bombers attacked the gas works, marshalling yard and railway viaducts; on 14 March 1945 the RAF wrecked the viaduct at Schildesche in the first use of its 10 tonne Grand Slam bomb. American troops entered in April 1945.</p><p>After the war Bielefeld was a headquarters town for the fighting command of the British Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, whose presence continued until the 1980s and ended with a final departure in 2020. On 1 January 1973 the city was merged with the surrounding district, taking in the south side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>; its population rose from 167,000 to 320,000 and its area from 48 to 259 km2.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bielefeld is in the Ostwestfalen-Lippe region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, with 342,952 inhabitants, mostly north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> in the Ravensberg Basin. Its university was founded in 1969, and the sociologist Niklas Luhmann was among its first professors. Companies based there include Dr. Oetker, and the Bethel Foundation is a major employer. Since 1994 the satirical Bielefeld conspiracy has claimed that the city does not exist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bielefeld” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their territory grew between the Teutoburg Forest and the Weser hills, and around 1214 Count Hermann IV founded the town of <b>Bielefeld</b> beneath the Sparrenburg castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">County of Ravensberg</a></li></ul></section>
